==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|state|ly}}. Compare {{l|en|stour}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsteɪtli/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-stately.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Of [[person|people]]: [[worthy]] of [[respect#Noun|respect]]; [[dignified#Adjective|dignified]], [[regal]].
#* '''1900''', [[w:Charles W. Chesnutt|Charles W. Chesnutt]], ''The House Behind the Cedars'', Chapter I,
#*: Warwick's first glance had revealed the fact that the young woman was strikingly handsome, with a '''stately''' beauty seldom encountered.
# Of [[movement]]: [[deliberate#Adjective|deliberate]], [[unhurried]]; dignified.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=An Own Goal on Gay Rights|newspaper={{w|The Economist}}|date=14 October 2010|passage=And much as they welcome his promise to repeal "don't ask, don't tell", they are dismayed by the '''stately''' pace and bungled tactics of his attempts to do so.}}
# [[grand#Adjective|Grand]], [[impressive]], [[imposing#Adjective|imposing]].
#* {{RQ:Coleridge Christabel|page=55|passage=In Xanadu did {{smallcaps|Kubla Khan}} / A '''stately''' pleasure-dome decree: / Where {{smallcaps|Alph}}, the sacred river, ran / Through caverns measureless to man / Down to a sunless sea.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|John le Carré}} [pseudonym; David John Moore Cornwell]|title={{w|A Perfect Spy}}|year=1986|passage=Flora is a good scout, a favourite with the jockeys on account of her '''stately''' breasts and the generous use she puts them to.}}

